+Reading: per-layer and per-op costs are similar in these samples (the fused k|v conv and the
+bounded sliding cache are slightly cheaper, the 8-expert gather slightly dearer than 6 + dense).
+Within the mlx-lm loop comparison, `async_eval` pipelining improves throughput by about 3%
+(20.9 vs 20.3 tok/s). Generation-loop overlap is one hypothesis for the end-to-end gap, but these
+measurements do not isolate its cause. In particular, mlx-vlm's 45.6 tok/s pipelined result is
+from hold 2 and its 46.9 ms synchronous step is from hold 3; their ratio cannot establish an
+overlap speedup on a machine whose throughput changed substantially between holds.
+
+The tiny-width dispatch benchmarks show graph build times of 2.0 vs 3.0 ms/token and similar
+launch costs, but do not isolate the bottleneck at real widths. Possible contributors include
+per-kernel encoding cost, buffer donation blocked by the one-step-ahead graph, and the
+command-buffer split at 50 ops. A quiet, same-hold comparison of synchronous and pipelined
+loops in both engines, or a CPU/GPU timeline, is needed to distinguish these hypotheses.
+Further kernel fusion is a candidate optimization that needs whole-model measurement.
 
